Mii Logical Architecture: A Categorical Framework for Conscious–Subconscious Coupling

ouadi Maakoul 

---

1. Prolegomena to a Formal Cognitive Architecture

The architecture of thought, long the purview of speculative philosophy and empirical psychology, has in recent decades come under the gaze of the formal sciences. The fundamental problem is not merely to describe what thinking is, but to provide a mathematizable model of how its conscious, deliberative aspect interacts with its subconscious, associative aspect—and how the two, though operating on seemingly incommensurable principles, achieve a coherent, global understanding. This dissertation constructs such a model.

The approach taken is category-theoretic. Category theory, originally developed as a unifying language for mathematics, has in the last two decades been fruitfully applied to cognitive science. In particular, the concept of an adjunction—a pair of functors that are, loosely speaking, inverse to one another in a weakened sense—has been proposed as a formal model for the relationship between conscious experience (qualia) and its verbal or behavioral reports. Tsuchiya, Saigo, and Phillips have advanced an “adjunction hypothesis” that identifies the qualia–report nexus as a pair of categories linked by a left and a right adjoint. Independently, the tradition of categorical compositionality, notably in the work of Phillips and Wilson, has demonstrated that the systematicity of human cognition—our capacity to recombine conceptual parts into novel wholes—is explained precisely by the existence of adjoint functors between representational categories.

This dissertation extends these insights in a new direction. Rather than focusing solely on the qualia–report relationship, it proposes a tripartite architecture consisting of:

· CR, the category of Conscious Reasoning, whose objects are logically structured states comprising propositions, contexts, reasoning traces, and attention distributions;
· SP, the category of Subconscious Pattern Processing, whose objects are associative networks of embeddings, heuristics, and priming vectors;
· Mii, a Meta-cognitive layer that couples the two, recording their translations and adjusting the balance between them.

Five functors mediate the interactions between these layers. Two fundamental adjunctions—Formalisation ⊣ Grounding and Pattern Lifting ⊣ Quotient—capture the bidirectional translation between logical and associative representations. Their composition yields an endofunctor  T  on the product category  \mathbf{CR} \times \mathbf{SP} . The central theorem of this work is that, under mild conditions, iterating  T  from an initial state converges, in the sense of the Scott topology, to a least fixed point: the state of stable understanding, where conscious and subconscious fully explain one another.

The meta-cognitive layer is then subjected to a sheaf-theoretic analysis. The assignment of meta-states to local sub-problems satisfies the gluing condition of a Grothendieck topology, a result that guarantees that local cognitive consistency can be uniquely lifted to global coherence. This line of reasoning resonates with recent work by Cacioppo, who has modeled percepts and valence as probabilistic structures in a presheaf topos, with affective valence inducing a Grothendieck topology that regulates which locally defined structures can be amalgamated.

Finally, the dissertation concludes with the Insight Y-Combinator (Section 6.5), an operational analogue of the cross-translation functor, and with convergence guarantees for the learning algorithms—stochastic gradient descent and evolutionary tournaments—that update the model’s numerical parameters.

The remainder of this chapter establishes the required mathematical preliminaries.

1.1 Categories, Functors, and Natural Transformations

The standard definitions are collected here for the reader’s convenience. For a full treatment, the reader is referred to Mac Lane’s Categories for the Working Mathematician and Borceux’s Handbook of Categorical Algebra.

Definition 1.1.1 (Category). A category  \mathcal{C}  consists of:

· a class of objects  \operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C}) ;
· for each pair  A, B \in \operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C}) , a set  \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)  of morphisms (or arrows);
· an associative composition operation  \circ: \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(B,C) \times \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B) \to \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,C) ;
· for each object  A , an identity morphism  \mathrm{id}_A \in \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,A)  satisfying the unit laws  f \circ \mathrm{id}_A = f  and  \mathrm{id}_B \circ f = f  for all  f: A \to B .

Definition 1.1.2 (Functor). A functor  F: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}  assigns to each object  A  of  \mathcal{C}  an object  F(A)  of  \mathcal{D} , and to each morphism  f: A \to B  a morphism  F(f): F(A) \to F(B) , preserving composition and identities:  F(g \circ f) = F(g) \circ F(f)  and  F(\mathrm{id}_A) = \mathrm{id}_{F(A)} .

Definition 1.1.3 (Natural Transformation). For functors  F, G: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D} , a natural transformation  \eta: F \Rightarrow G  is a family of morphisms  \{\eta_X: F(X) \to G(X)\}_{X \in \operatorname{Ob}(\mathcal{C})}  such that, for every morphism  f: X \to Y  in  \mathcal{C} , the diagram

\begin{CD}
F(X) @>{\eta_X}>> G(X)\\
@V{F(f)}VV @VV{G(f)}V\\
F(Y) @>{\eta_Y}>> G(Y)
\end{CD}

commutes.

1.2 Adjunctions

Definition 1.2.1 (Adjunction). An adjunction between categories  \mathcal{C}  and  \mathcal{D}  consists of a pair of functors  F: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}  and  G: \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{C} , together with natural transformations  \eta: \mathrm{Id}_{\mathcal{C}} \Rightarrow G \circ F  (the unit) and  \varepsilon: F \circ G \Rightarrow \mathrm{Id}_{\mathcal{D}}  (the counit), such that the triangle identities hold:

\varepsilon_{F(A)} \circ F(\eta_A) = \mathrm{id}_{F(A)}, \qquad G(\varepsilon_B) \circ \eta_{G(B)} = \mathrm{id}_{G(B)}.

We write  F \dashv G  and say that  F  is left adjoint to  G  (equivalently,  G  is right adjoint to  F ).

Adjunctions are pervasive in mathematics. Their relevance to cognition has been argued persuasively by Phillips and Wilson, who observe that the systematicity of human thought—the fact that anyone who can think “John loves Mary” can also think “Mary loves John”—is a structural property that falls out of the existence of an adjoint pair of functors between a category of syntactic forms and a category of semantic representations. The classic example in that work is the adjunction between the tensor product and the internal hom in a closed monoidal category, which models the composition of complex representations from simpler ones.

In the present framework, adjunctions serve a more specific purpose: they model the translation between two fundamentally different modes of representation—logical-propositional on the one hand, associative-pattern-based on the other—that cannot be reduced to one another but maintain a coherent, bidirectional relationship.

1.3 Complete Partial Orders and Fixed Points

Definition 1.3.1 (Complete Partial Order). A poset  (D, \sqsubseteq)  with a least element  \bot  is a complete partial order (cpo) if every directed subset has a supremum.

Definition 1.3.2 (Scott Continuity). A function  f: D \to E  between cpos is Scott-continuous if it is monotone and preserves directed suprema: for every directed subset  X \subseteq D ,  f(\bigsqcup X) = \bigsqcup f(X) .

Theorem 1.3.3 (Kleene Fixed-Point Theorem). Let  f: D \to D  be a Scott-continuous function on a cpo  D  with bottom  \bot . Then  f  has a least fixed point, given by

\operatorname{lfp}(f) = \bigsqcup_{n \geq 0} f^n(\bot).

The Kleene fixed-point theorem is a standard tool in denotational semantics and domain theory. It guarantees that the iterative process of going back and forth between conscious and subconscious representations, which will be formalized in Chapter 5, does not spiral into chaos but converges to a well-defined equilibrium.

1.4 Grothendieck Topologies and Sheaves

The final piece of mathematical machinery concerns the global consistency of the meta-cognitive layer.

Definition 1.4.1 (Coverage). A coverage on a category  \mathcal{X}  assigns to each object  U  a collection of families of morphisms  \{U_i \to U\} , called covering families, satisfying suitable stability conditions.

Definition 1.4.2 (Sheaf). A presheaf  \mathcal{F}: \mathcal{X}^{\mathrm{op}} \to \mathbf{Set}  is a sheaf with respect to a coverage if, for every covering family  \{U_i \to U\} , the diagram

\mathcal{F}(U) \longrightarrow \prod_i \mathcal{F}(U_i) \rightrightarrows \prod_{i,j} \mathcal{F}(U_i \times_U U_j)

is an equalizer in  \mathbf{Set} . This condition captures the idea that a global section (an element of  \mathcal{F}(U) ) is uniquely determined by a compatible family of local sections (elements of the  \mathcal{F}(U_i) ).

The sheaf condition has been used in cognitive science to model the “patching” of local knowledge into a coherent global picture, a line of inquiry inaugurated by Phillips in the context of categorical compositionality and recently extended by Cacioppo to the modeling of percepts and affective valence in a topos-theoretic setting. In the present architecture, the sheaf property of the meta-cognitive layer (Chapter 6) encodes the condition that local cognitive states—states of understanding relative to a particular sub-problem—can be uniquely glued into a globally coherent understanding, provided they agree on overlaps.

