2. The Three Cognitive Categories

2.1 The Conscious Layer: CR

The conscious layer models deliberate, logical reasoning. Its objects are structured states that capture what an agent is explicitly attending to and reasoning about at a given moment.

Definition 2.1.1 (Object of CR). An object  C \in \mathbf{CR}  is a tuple

C = (P, \mathrm{Ctx}, \tau, \mathcal{W}),

where

·  P \subseteq \mathrm{Prop}  is a finite set of propositions;
·  \mathrm{Ctx} = (\mathrm{World}, \mathrm{Goal}, \mathrm{Constraint})  is a context triple encoding the agent’s world model, its current goal, and any constraints on action;
·  \tau \in \mathrm{Trace}^*  is a finite reasoning trace, recording the inference steps that have led to the current state;
·  \mathcal{W}: P \to [0,1]  is an attention-weight distribution satisfying  \sum_{p \in P} \mathcal{W}(p) = 1 .

The presence of the attention-weight distribution reflects a cognitive reality: even within conscious deliberation, not all propositions are equally salient. The distribution  \mathcal{W}  models the differential allocation of attentional resources.

Definition 2.1.2 (Morphism of CR). A morphism  f: C_1 \to C_2  is a tuple

f = (A, B, \mathrm{just}(f), \mathrm{conf}(f)),

where

·  A \subseteq P_1  and  B \subseteq P_2  are the source and target subsets of propositions linked by the reasoning step;
·  \mathrm{just}(f)  is a justification—a formal proof, a set of inference rules, or a certificate that the transition from  A  to  B  is valid under the given context;
·  \mathrm{conf}(f) \in [0,1]  is a confidence value.

Composition. For  f: C_1 \to C_2  and  g: C_2 \to C_3 ,

g \circ f = \bigl( A, C,\; \mathrm{just}(g) \wedge \mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f), \mathrm{conf}(g)) \cdot \mathrm{coh}(f,g) \bigr),

where  \mathrm{coh}(f,g)  is a coherence factor, defined as the Jaccard similarity of the justification sets:

\mathrm{coh}(f,g) = \frac{|\mathrm{just}(f) \cap \mathrm{just}(g)|}{|\mathrm{just}(f) \cup \mathrm{just}(g)|},

with the convention  \mathrm{coh} = 1  when both justifications are empty. The identity on  C  is  \mathrm{id}_C = (P, P, \text{empty justification}, 1.0) .

The use of a coherence factor on justifications is a novel contribution of this architecture. It acknowledges that the composition of two reasoning steps may introduce an additional loss of confidence if the justifications are not fully compatible. This is a formalization of the intuitive notion that chaining together tentative inferences can compound uncertainty.

The proof that CR is a well-defined category is provided in the main body: associativity follows from the associativity of conjunction and a lemma establishing the cyclic invariance of the coherence factors, and the unit laws are immediate.

2.2 The Subconscious Layer: SP

The subconscious layer models associative, pattern-based processing. Unlike CR, which is propositional and logical, SP is continuous and similarity-based. Its objects are states of an associative network.

Definition 2.2.1 (Object of SP). An object  S \in \mathbf{SP}  is a tuple

S = (E, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{H}, \mathcal{B}),

where

·  E \in \mathbb{R}^d  is an embedding vector;
·  \mathcal{A}  is a weighted association graph with vertex set  V_{\mathcal{A}}  and symmetric weight function  w: V_{\mathcal{A}} \times V_{\mathcal{A}} \to [0,1] ;
·  \mathcal{H}  is a heuristic database, storing patterns that have proven useful in past processing;
·  \mathcal{B} \in \mathbb{R}^k  is a priming vector, which biases the network toward certain patterns in a context-dependent way.

Definition 2.2.2 (Morphism of SP). A morphism  f: S_1 \to S_2  is a tuple

f = (X, Y, \mathcal{T}_f, \mathrm{sim}_f),

where  \mathcal{T}_f: V_{\mathcal{A}_1} \to V_{\mathcal{A}_2}  maps vertices, and  \mathrm{sim}_f \in [0,1]  is a similarity strength.

Composition:

g \circ f = (X, Z,\; \mathcal{T}_g \circ \mathcal{T}_f,\; \mathrm{sim}_f \cdot \mathrm{sim}_g \cdot \mathrm{compat}(f,g)),

with  \mathrm{compat}(f,g) \in [0,1]  a compatibility factor. This makes SP a category enriched over the monoidal category  ([0,1], \times, 1) . The enrichment structure is significant: it captures the intuition that subconscious transitions are not all-or-nothing but come in degrees, and that the degree of a composite transition is the product (a t-norm) of the degrees of its factors.

2.3 The Meta-Cognitive Layer: Mii

The meta-cognitive layer is the coupling agent between CR and SP. It does not merely record the current state of each layer; it actively manages their translation and the balance between them.

Definition 2.3.1 (Object of Mii). An object  M \in \mathbf{Mii}  is a quintuple

M = (C, S, \mathcal{T}, \alpha, \beta, \gamma),

where

·  C \in \mathbf{CR}  is a conscious state;
·  S \in \mathbf{SP}  is a subconscious state;
·  \mathcal{T}  is a bidirectional translation table consisting of triples  (c, s, w)  with  c \in P_C ,  s \in V_{\mathcal{A}_S} , and coupling weight  w \in [0,1] ;
·  \alpha, \beta, \gamma \geq 0  with  \alpha + \beta + \gamma = 1  are the balance coefficients:  \alpha  is the weight on the conscious layer,  \beta  on the subconscious, and  \gamma  on the meta-cognitive layer itself.

The introduction of dynamic balance coefficients is a distinctive feature of the Mii architecture. It permits the system to shift its cognitive stance: a high  \alpha  corresponds to a predominantly analytical mode, a high  \beta  to an intuitive, pattern-driven mode, and a high  \gamma  to a self-reflective, meta-cognitive stance.

Definition 2.3.2 (Morphism of Mii). A morphism  \varphi: M_1 \to M_2  is a pair

\varphi = (M_1, M_2, \mathrm{Adj}, \mathrm{insight}),

where  \mathrm{Adj}: (\alpha_1, \beta_1, \gamma_1) \to (\alpha_2, \beta_2, \gamma_2)  adjusts the balance coefficients, and  \mathrm{insight} \in [0,1]  quantifies the cognitive gain of the transition.

