4. The Two Fundamental Adjunctions

This chapter contains the central structural results of the architecture: the proofs that the two pairs of functors,  (F, G)  and  (P, Q) , form adjunctions. The proofs are constructive, exhibiting the natural bijections between hom-sets, the units, and the counits, and verifying the triangle identities.

4.1 Theorem:  F \dashv G  (Formalisation ⊣ Grounding)

Theorem 4.1.1. There exists an adjunction  F: \mathbf{CR} \to \mathbf{Mii} \dashv G: \mathbf{Mii} \to \mathbf{CR} .

Proof (Sketch). The natural bijection

\Phi_{C,M}: \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), M) \longrightarrow \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(M))

is constructed as follows. A morphism  \varphi = (F(C), M, \mathrm{Adj}, \mathrm{insight})  in Mii induces, via the adjustment  \mathrm{Adj} , a mapping from the translation table of  F(C)  to that of  M . This, in turn, defines for each proposition  p \in P  a grounded proposition in  G(M) . The conscious morphism  \Phi(\varphi)  takes the subset of propositions in  C  that are active in  \varphi , transfers them to the corresponding grounded propositions, and assigns confidence equal to  \mathrm{insight} .

The unit  \eta_C: C \to G(F(C))  is the inclusion of  C  into the state enriched by the high-confidence translations of patterns extracted from its own trace. The counit  \varepsilon_M: F(G(M)) \to M  adjusts the translation table of  F(G(M))  to match that of  M  and resets the balance coefficients.

The first triangle identity,  \varepsilon_{F(C)} \circ F(\eta_C) = \mathrm{id}_{F(C)} , reflects the fact that formalizing  C , grounding it, and then applying the counit discards exactly the additions made by the round-trip, restoring the original state. The second identity,  G(\varepsilon_M) \circ \eta_{G(M)} = \mathrm{id}_{G(M)} , is the dual verification. ∎

4.2 Theorem:  P \dashv Q  (Pattern Lifting ⊣ Quotient)

Theorem 4.2.1. There exists an adjunction  P: \mathbf{SP} \to \mathbf{Mii} \dashv Q: \mathbf{Mii} \to \mathbf{SP} .

Proof (Sketch). The proof is entirely dual to that of Theorem 4.1.1, with the roles of propositions and pattern vertices exchanged. The unit  \eta_S: S \to Q(P(S))  embeds the subconscious state into the quotient of its lifted version; the counit  \varepsilon_M: P(Q(M)) \to M  collapses the round-trip by identifying the trivial propositions introduced by lifting with their counterparts in  M . The triangle identities are verified by the same structural reasoning. ∎

4.3 Significance of the Adjunctions

The two adjunctions encapsulate the fundamental asymmetry of the conscious–subconscious relationship. The left adjoints ( F  and  P ) are generative: they add structure, extracting patterns from traces and creating propositional interpretations of vertices. The right adjoints ( G  and  Q ) are selective: they filter and ground, admitting only high-confidence translations into the conscious or subconscious layers. This asymmetry mirrors the cognitive intuition that formalization and pattern recognition are expansive processes, while grounding and encoding require selection to avoid combinatorial explosion.

The use of adjunctions in this context is in the spirit of the adjunction hypothesis advanced by Tsuchiya, Saigo, and Phillips. In their formulation, the adjunction captures the nuanced relationship between qualia and reports: two categories that cannot be considered equivalent, yet maintain a coherent bidirectional link. Here, the same formal mechanism applies to the relationship between two cognitive subsystems, suggesting a unified categorical treatment of various levels of the mind.

