7. The Derived Galois Connection

The adjunctions of Chapter 4 give rise to a composite adjunction between the conscious and subconscious categories directly, bypassing the meta-cognitive layer.

Definition 7.1. Define

L = Q \circ F: \mathbf{CR} \to \mathbf{SP}, \qquad R = G \circ P: \mathbf{SP} \to \mathbf{CR}.

Theorem 7.1 (Galois Connection). For any  C \in \mathbf{CR}  and  S \in \mathbf{SP} ,

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R(S).

Proof. From the adjunctions  F \dashv G  and  P \dashv Q ,

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L(C), S) &= \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(Q(F(C)), S) \\
&\cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), P(S)) \quad \text{(by } P \dashv Q\text{)} \\
&\cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(P(S))) \quad \text{(by } F \dashv G\text{)} \\
&= \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R(S)).
\end{aligned}

With the identification of the partial order  \sqsubseteq  with the existence of a morphism, the bijection of hom-sets is exactly the stated equivalence. ∎

The Galois connection characterizes the stable-understanding pair  (C^*, S^*)  as a mutual fixed point:  L(C^*) = S^*  and  R(S^*) = C^* . This is the structural expression of the intuitive notion that, in the state of stable understanding, the conscious and subconscious are coupled by a pair of mutually inverse (in the posetal sense) translations.

