8. Convergence of Learning Algorithms

The categories and functors of the preceding chapters provide the static architecture. For the architecture to be realizable in practice, the numerical parameters—embeddings, weights, heuristic tables—must be learnable from data. This chapter provides the convergence guarantees.

8.1 Stochastic Gradient Descent

Theorem 8.1.1 (SGD Convergence). Let  \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{z}[\ell(\theta, z)]  be a loss function with a Lipschitz-continuous gradient. Assume the gradient estimates  \nabla \ell(\theta_t, z_t)  are unbiased with bounded variance  \sigma^2 , and the step sizes  \alpha_t  satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions:

\sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \alpha_t = \infty, \qquad \sum_{t=1}^{\infty} \alpha_t^2 < \infty.

Then the iterates  \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \alpha_t \nabla \ell(\theta_t, z_t)  converge almost surely to a stationary point of  \mathcal{L} .

Proof. This is the classical Robbins–Monro theorem. The conditions ensure that the associated ordinary differential equation  \dot{\theta} = -\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta)  has the iterates as asymptotic pseudo-trajectories, and standard Lyapunov arguments yield convergence. ∎

8.2 Evolutionary Tournament

Theorem 8.2.1 (Evolutionary Convergence). An elitist genetic algorithm on a finite search space, with positive mutation probability  \mu > 0  applied to each individual per generation, converges in probability to the global optimum of the fitness function.

Proof. The algorithm defines a Markov chain on the space of populations. Elitism ensures that the best fitness is never lost, and positive mutation guarantees that every point in the search space is reachable. The chain is therefore absorbing at states containing a global optimum, and standard Markov chain arguments yield exponential decay of the probability of not having found an optimum. ∎

