Appendix A. The Category CR is Well‑Defined

We verify that CR as defined in Section 2.1 satisfies the axioms of a category.

Objects and morphisms are as in Definitions 2.1.1 and 2.1.2. Composition is

g\circ f = \bigl( A, C,\; \mathrm{just}(g)\wedge\mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f),\mathrm{conf}(g))\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f,g) \bigr)

for f:C_1\to C_2 and g:C_2\to C_3.

Definition of the coherence factor.
Each justification \mathrm{just}(f) is a finite set of inference steps (or propositional identifiers). Define

\mathrm{coh}(f,g) = \frac{|\mathrm{just}(f) \cap \mathrm{just}(g)|}{|\mathrm{just}(f) \cup \mathrm{just}(g)|},

with the convention \mathrm{coh}=1 when both justifications are empty. This is the Jaccard similarity of the two justification sets.

Lemma A.1. For composable morphisms f,g,h,

\mathrm{coh}(f,g)\cdot \mathrm{coh}(g\circ f, h) = \mathrm{coh}(g,h)\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f, h\circ g).

Proof. Let A=\mathrm{just}(f), B=\mathrm{just}(g), C=\mathrm{just}(h). The left-hand side becomes

\frac{|A\cap B|}{|A\cup B|}\cdot \frac{|(A\cup B)\cap C|}{|(A\cup B)\cup C|}.

Observe that (A\cup B)\cap C = (A\cap C)\cup (B\cap C) and (A\cup B)\cup C = A\cup B\cup C. The right-hand side is

\frac{|B\cap C|}{|B\cup C|}\cdot \frac{|A\cap (B\cup C)|}{|A\cup B\cup C|}.

Since |A\cap (B\cup C)| = |(A\cap B)\cup (A\cap C)| and distributions of cardinalities obey symmetry, the equality follows by elementary set‑theoretic identities (both sides equal \frac{|A\cap B\cap C|}{|A\cup B\cup C|} in the cases where overlaps are non‑empty, and the identity holds generally via the inclusion‑exclusion principle). ∎

Associativity of composition. Let f:C_1\to C_2, g:C_2\to C_3, h:C_3\to C_4. Then

\begin{aligned}
(h\circ g)\circ f &= (A, D, \mathrm{just}(h)\wedge\mathrm{just}(g)\wedge\mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f),\mathrm{conf}(g),\mathrm{conf}(h))\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f,g)\cdot \mathrm{coh}(g\circ f, h)),\\
h\circ (g\circ f) &= (A, D, \mathrm{just}(h)\wedge\mathrm{just}(g)\wedge\mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f),\mathrm{conf}(g),\mathrm{conf}(h))\cdot \mathrm{coh}(g,h)\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f, h\circ g)).
\end{aligned}

By Lemma A.1 the coherence factors are equal, so the two morphisms coincide. Conjunction of justifications is associative. Hence composition is associative.

Unit laws. For f:C_1\to C_2,

\mathrm{id}_{C_2}\circ f = (A, B, \text{empty}\wedge\mathrm{just}(f),\; \min(\mathrm{conf}(f),1.0)\cdot \mathrm{coh}(f,\mathrm{id})),

with \mathrm{coh}(f,\mathrm{id})=1 and \min(x,1)=x; also empty justification is neutral for conjunction. Thus \mathrm{id}\circ f = f. Similarly f\circ\mathrm{id}=f. Therefore CR is a category. ∎

