Appendix G. Scott‑Continuity of T (Lemma 5.4)

We show T preserves directed suprema. Let \{(C_i,S_i)\}_{i\in I} be a directed subset with supremum (\bar{C},\bar{S}).

Consider the first component of T: G(P(S_i)). The functor P lifts S_i to a meta‑state with propositions corresponding to vertices. Since S_i \sqsubseteq S_j means V_{\mathcal{A}_i}\subseteq V_{\mathcal{A}_j}, lifting larger sets yields more propositions; thus P is monotone. The functor G grounds a meta‑state, adding only high‑confidence translations. Both commute with directed unions: extracting patterns from the union of association graphs yields the union of the extractions, and grounding the union of meta‑states is the union of the groundings. Therefore

\sup_i G(P(S_i)) = G(P(\sup_i S_i)) = G(P(\bar{S})).

Analogously, for the second component Q(F(C_i)),

\sup_i Q(F(C_i)) = Q(F(\sup_i C_i)) = Q(F(\bar{C})).

Hence

T(\sup_i (C_i,S_i)) = \bigl( G(P(\bar{S})), Q(F(\bar{C})) \bigr) = \sup_i \bigl( G(P(S_i)), Q(F(C_i)) \bigr) = \sup_i T(C_i,S_i).

The monotonicity of T is already implicit in preservation of directed suprema. Therefore T is Scott‑continuous. ∎

