Appendix H. Proof of the Kleene Fixed‑Point Theorem (Classic)

Let f:D\to D be Scott‑continuous on a cpo D with bottom \bot. Define x_0 = \bot, x_{n+1} = f(x_n).

· Monotonicity of f: For any x\sqsubseteq y, the set \{x,y\} is directed, so f(x\sqcup y) = f(x) \sqcup f(y); but since x\sqcup y = y, we have f(y) = f(x)\sqcup f(y), which implies f(x)\sqsubseteq f(y). Hence f is monotone.
· Chain property: Since \bot \sqsubseteq f(\bot) and monotonicity gives f^n(\bot) \sqsubseteq f^{n+1}(\bot), the sequence \{x_n\} is an increasing chain, hence directed.
· Existence of supremum: Let x^* = \bigsqcup_n x_n (exists in cpo).
· Fixed point: By Scott‑continuity,
  f(x^*) = f\bigl( \bigsqcup_n x_n \bigr) = \bigsqcup_n f(x_n) = \bigsqcup_n x_{n+1} = x^*.
· Leastness: If y is any fixed point, then x_0 = \bot \sqsubseteq y. Assuming x_n \sqsubseteq y, by monotonicity x_{n+1}=f(x_n)\sqsubseteq f(y)=y. By induction all x_n\sqsubseteq y, so x^* = \bigsqcup_n x_n \sqsubseteq y. Thus x^* is the least fixed point. ∎

