Appendix I. Sheaf Proof Details (Theorem 6.2)

Recall \mathfrak{M}(U) = \{ M_U \in \mathbf{Mii} \mid M_U \text{ is a valid meta‑state for problem } U \}. For a covering \{U_i\to U\}, we must show that

\mathfrak{M}(U) \longrightarrow \prod_i \mathfrak{M}(U_i) \rightrightarrows \prod_{i,j} \mathfrak{M}(U_i\cap U_j)

is an equaliser diagram.

I.1 Locality (injectivity of the left map)

Suppose s,t\in \mathfrak{M}(U) satisfy s|_{U_i} = t|_{U_i} for all i. The components of a meta‑state are the conscious tuple (P_U,\mathrm{Ctx}_U,\tau_U,\mathcal{W}_U), the subconscious tuple (E,\mathcal{A},\mathcal{H},\mathcal{B}), the translation table \mathcal{T}, and balance coefficients.

Restriction to U_i means:

· For the conscious part, we keep only propositions that are relevant to sub‑problem U_i; the context and trace are filtered accordingly.
· For the subconscious part, we restrict the association graph to vertices that are active in the context U_i; the embedding is projected (or restricted) to the subspace relevant to that sub‑problem.

The covering family is jointly surjective on the space of propositions and vertices relevant to U. Therefore, if the restrictions agree on every U_i, each global proposition must belong to some U_i, and equality of restrictions forces equality of the proposition contents. Similarly for vertices. The translation tables and balance coefficients must also coincide, because any mismatch would manifest in some U_i (the cover includes all contexts). Thus s = t.

I.2 Gluing (surjectivity onto the equaliser)

Let \{s_i \in \mathfrak{M}(U_i)\} be a compatible family: s_i|_{U_i\cap U_j} = s_j|_{U_i\cap U_j} for all i,j.

Conscious component. Let P_i be the proposition set of s_i. On overlaps, the restricted proposition sets are identical. Define P_U' = \bigcup_i P_i. If two propositions from different i,j are logically equivalent relative to the overlap context (which is itself a sub‑problem), we identify them. Because compatibility guarantees no contradictory logical relations (any inconsistency would appear in some U_i), the deductive closure of P_U' is consistent. Take P_U as this closure. The context \mathrm{Ctx}_U is the join of the individual contexts; the trace is the concatenation of the traces, filling in bridging steps if needed.

Subconscious component.

· Embeddings: On each U_i, we have an embedding vector E_i. Compatibility means that the embedding restricted to the subspace for U_i\cap U_j is the same for both s_i and s_j. We can patch these local embeddings into a global embedding by considering the direct sum of the vector spaces and taking the quotient by equality on overlaps; this is well‑defined because of compatibility.
· Association graphs: Vertices are unioned; two vertices from different s_i are identified if they are mapped to each other under the canonical transition maps on the overlap. Edge weights are defined as the maximum weight from any covering piece containing both vertices; compatibility ensures that on overlaps the weights agree, so this maximum is well‑defined. This yields a graph \mathcal{A}_U.

Translation table. The union of all triples, identifying triples that are determined to be equal by the overlap identifications of propositions and vertices. Compatibility forces equal weights on identified triples.

Balance coefficients. The balance coefficients (\alpha_i,\beta_i,\gamma_i) of the local states must be equal on overlaps because the restriction maps for balance are the identity (balance is a global meta‑parameter). Hence all s_i have the same balance coefficients; take that common tuple.

The constructed global section s restricts to s_i by construction: the restriction operations discard propositions and vertices that do not belong to the sub‑problem, which are exactly those that came from other U_j. Thus the diagram is an equaliser, and \mathfrak{M} is a sheaf. ∎

