Appendix J. Proof of the Derived Galois Connection (Theorem 7.1)

Recall L = Q\circ F : \mathbf{CR}\to\mathbf{SP} and R = G\circ P : \mathbf{SP}\to\mathbf{CR}. We prove the equivalence

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R(S).

Using the two adjunctions:

\begin{aligned}
&\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(L(C), S) \\
&= \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{SP}}(Q(F(C)), S) \\
&\cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{Mii}}(F(C), P(S)) \quad \text{(by } P\dashv Q\text{, the natural bijection)}\\
&\cong \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, G(P(S))) = \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{CR}}(C, R(S)) \quad \text{(by } F\dashv G\text{)}.
\end{aligned}

In our setting, the partial order \sqsubseteq is defined by the existence of a morphism: X \sqsubseteq Y iff \mathrm{Hom}(X,Y)\neq \varnothing. Therefore the above bijection of hom‑sets translates directly to:

L(C) \sqsubseteq S \iff C \sqsubseteq R(S).

Thus L and R form a (posetal) adjunction, i.e. a Galois connection. ∎

