Appendix K. Convergence of Learning Algorithms (Theorems 8.1 and 8.2)

K.1 Stochastic Gradient Descent

Theorem K.1 (SGD Convergence). Let \mathcal{L}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_z[\ell(\theta,z)] be a loss function such that \nabla\mathcal{L} is Lipschitz continuous with constant L. Suppose \nabla\ell(\theta,z) is an unbiased estimator of \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta) with \mathbb{E}[\|\nabla\ell(\theta,z) - \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta)\|^2] \le \sigma^2. Choose step sizes a_t > 0 satisfying the Robbins–Monro conditions:

\sum_{t=1}^\infty a_t = \infty, \qquad \sum_{t=1}^\infty a_t^2 < \infty.

Then the recursion \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - a_t \nabla\ell(\theta_t, z_t) yields \nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_t) \to 0 almost surely, and the sequence \{\theta_t\} converges to a stationary point of \mathcal{L}.

Proof sketch. This is a standard result in stochastic approximation. The Robbins–Monro conditions ensure that the discrete‑time process tracks the ordinary differential equation \dot{\theta} = -\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta). Lipschitz continuity of the gradient guarantees existence and uniqueness of solutions, and a Lyapunov function (the loss itself) decreases along trajectories. The almost‑sure convergence follows from martingale arguments; see Robbins & Monro (1951) and subsequent textbook treatments. ∎

K.2 Evolutionary Tournament

Theorem K.2 (Evolutionary Convergence). Consider an elitist genetic algorithm operating on a finite search space, with tournament selection, and a positive mutation probability \mu > 0 applied independently to each individual per generation. The algorithm converges in probability to the set of globally optimal solutions.

Proof sketch. The algorithm defines a Markov chain on the state space of populations. Elitism guarantees that the maximum fitness in the population is non‑decreasing. Positive mutation ensures that any point in the search space can be reached from any other with positive probability in one generation. Thus the chain is absorbing with the set of populations containing a global optimum as the only recurrent class. Standard Markov chain limit theorems then imply that the probability that the population does not contain a global optimum decays exponentially with the number of generations, proving convergence in probability. ∎

